## Linux DRM - Sharing Buffers with PRIME and dma-buf
```require
- Kernel: Based on Linux v6.19 source
- Library: libdrm 2.4.129
- Prerequisites: Linux DRM Part 4 GEM
```

### Introduction
| Part | Topic |
|---|---|
| Part 1 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-26-post-linux-drm-01-architecture">Architecture and the Big Picture</a> |
| Part 2 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-27-post-linux-drm-02-register">Core Initialization and Driver Registration</a> |
| Part 3 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-28-post-linux-drm-03-open-ioctl">open() and ioctl() Code Flow</a> |
| Part 4 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-29-post-linux-drm-04-gem-dumb-mmap">GEM: handle, dumb buffer, mmap</a> |

The GEM handles we saw in Part 4 are only valid within a `drm_file`. For a display server (compositor) to receive a buffer rendered by an application, or for a camera or video decoder to read a buffer created by a GPU, <b>a sharing mechanism passable via an fd</b> is required. This article walks through that mechanism, <mark>dma-buf</mark>, and <mark>PRIME</mark>, the DRM feature that bridges GEM and dma-buf.  


```adcard
```

### PRIME: Sharing Buffers Between Processes and Devices
GEM handles are meaningful only within a `drm_file` and cannot be passed directly to other processes. For a display server (compositor) to receive buffers drawn by applications, or for a camera/video decoder to read buffers produced by the GPU, <b>a sharing mechanism that can be passed as an fd</b> is necessary. Linux's answer to this is <mark>dma-buf</mark>, and the DRM feature bridging GEM and dma-buf is called <mark>PRIME</mark>.  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "dma-buf: exporter · importer · attachment"
}
---
flowchart TB
    subgraph EXP["Exporter (e.g., GPU Driver A)"]
        OBJ["drm_gem_object"] --- DB["struct dma_buf<br/>ops = drm_gem_prime_dmabuf_ops"]
    end
    DB --> FD["dma-buf fd<br/>Can be passed to another process via SCM_RIGHTS"]
    FD -->|"dma_buf_attach<br/>dma_buf_map_attachment"| ATT
    subgraph IMP["Importer (different driver or same driver)"]
        ATT["dma_buf_attachment"] --> SGT["sg_table<br/>(list of physical pages)"] --> OBJ2["drm_gem_object (import)"]
    end
```
- The <b>exporter</b> wraps its buffer into a `struct dma_buf` and exposes it as an `fd`.
- The <b>importer</b> calls `dma_buf_attach()` with that fd, then `dma_buf_map_attachment()` to obtain an <b>sg_table (list of physical pages)</b> and creates its own buffer object.
- Data is not copied. Both sides view the <b>same physical pages</b>.

#### export: handle → dma-buf fd
The user space calls `DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD`. Because this ioctl sets `DRM_RENDER_ALLOW` in its flags, it can also be called on render nodes (see the ioctl table in Part 3).  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_prime_handle_to_fd_ioctl() (L533-549)"
}
---
int drm_prime_handle_to_fd_ioctl(struct drm_device *dev, void *data,  // [!code highlight]
				 struct drm_file *file_priv)
{
	struct drm_prime_handle *args = data;

	if (args->flags & ~(DRM_CLOEXEC | DRM_RDWR))
		return -EINVAL;

	if (dev->driver->prime_handle_to_fd) {  // [!code highlight]
		return dev->driver->prime_handle_to_fd(dev, file_priv,  // [!code highlight]
						       args->handle, args->flags,
						       &args->fd);
	}
	return drm_gem_prime_handle_to_fd(dev, file_priv, args->handle,  // [!code highlight]
					  args->flags, &args->fd);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L533-L549" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L533-L549</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_handle_to_fd() (L510-530)"
}
---
int drm_gem_prime_handle_to_fd(struct drm_device *dev,
			       struct drm_file *file_priv, uint32_t handle,
			       uint32_t flags,
			       int *prime_fd)
{
	struct dma_buf *dmabuf;
	int fd = get_unused_fd_flags(flags);  // [!code highlight]

	if (fd < 0)
		return fd;

	dmabuf = drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf(dev, file_priv, handle, flags);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(dmabuf)) {
		put_unused_fd(fd);
		return PTR_ERR(dmabuf);
	}

	fd_install(fd, dmabuf->file);  // [!code highlight]
	*prime_fd = fd;
	return 0;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L510-L530" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L510-L530</a> (Linux v6.19)

It reserves an `fd`, acquires the dma-buf, and associates it with that fd using `fd_install()`. The key function responsible for acquiring the dma-buf is <mark>drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf()</mark>.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf() (L431-494)"
}
---
struct dma_buf *drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf(struct drm_device *dev,
			       struct drm_file *file_priv, uint32_t handle,
			       uint32_t flags)
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj;
	int ret = 0;
	struct dma_buf *dmabuf;

	mutex_lock(&file_priv->prime.lock);
	obj = drm_gem_object_lookup(file_priv, handle);  // [!code highlight]
	if (!obj)  {
		dmabuf = ERR_PTR(-ENOENT);
		goto out_unlock;
	}

	dmabuf = drm_prime_lookup_buf_by_handle(&file_priv->prime, handle);  // [!code highlight]
	if (dmabuf) {
		get_dma_buf(dmabuf);
		goto out;
	}

	mutex_lock(&dev->object_name_lock);

	if (obj->import_attach) {  // [!code highlight]
		dmabuf = obj->import_attach->dmabuf;  // [!code highlight]
		get_dma_buf(dmabuf);
		goto out_have_obj;
	}

	if (obj->dma_buf) {  // [!code highlight]
		get_dma_buf(obj->dma_buf);  // [!code highlight]
		dmabuf = obj->dma_buf;  // [!code highlight]
		goto out_have_obj;
	}

	dmabuf = export_and_register_object(dev, obj, flags);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(dmabuf)) {
		mutex_unlock(&dev->object_name_lock);
		goto out;
	}

out_have_obj:

	ret = drm_prime_add_buf_handle(&file_priv->prime,
				       dmabuf, handle);
	mutex_unlock(&dev->object_name_lock);
	if (ret) {
		dma_buf_put(dmabuf);
		dmabuf = ERR_PTR(ret);
	}
out:
	drm_gem_object_put(obj);
out_unlock:
	mutex_unlock(&file_priv->prime.lock);
	return dmabuf;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L431-L494" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L431-L494</a> (Linux v6.19)

The branching logic inside this function forms the core of this section:  

1. `drm_gem_object_lookup()`: Looks up the object by handle.
2. <mark>If already exported in this file</mark> (`drm_prime_lookup_buf_by_handle`), it returns that dma-buf as-is.
3. If it is an imported object, it returns the original dma-buf (`obj->import_attach->dmabuf`).
4. If `obj->dma_buf` already exists, it is reused.
5. <mark>A new one is created</mark> only when none of the above exist (`export_and_register_object`).

Thus, <b>exporting the same handle multiple times still yields the exact same dma-buf</b>. This explains why `export #1` and `export #2` point to the identical inode in the example below.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - export_and_register_object() (L375-407)"
}
---
static struct dma_buf *export_and_register_object(struct drm_device *dev,
						  struct drm_gem_object *obj,
						  uint32_t flags)
{
	struct dma_buf *dmabuf;

	if (obj->handle_count == 0) {
		dmabuf = ERR_PTR(-ENOENT);
		return dmabuf;
	}

	if (obj->funcs && obj->funcs->export)  // [!code highlight]
		dmabuf = obj->funcs->export(obj, flags);  // [!code highlight]
	else
		dmabuf = drm_gem_prime_export(obj, flags);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(dmabuf)) {
		return dmabuf;
	}

	obj->dma_buf = dmabuf;  // [!code highlight]
	get_dma_buf(obj->dma_buf);  // [!code highlight]

	return dmabuf;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L375-L407" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L375-L407</a> (Linux v6.19)

If `obj->funcs->export` exists, the backend implementation is used; otherwise, it falls back to the core default `drm_gem_prime_export()`.  


```adcard
```


```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_export() (L916-931)"
}
---
struct dma_buf *drm_gem_prime_export(struct drm_gem_object *obj,
				     int flags)
{
	struct drm_device *dev = obj->dev;
	struct dma_buf_export_info exp_info = {
		.exp_name = KBUILD_MODNAME,
		.owner = dev->driver->fops->owner,
		.ops = &drm_gem_prime_dmabuf_ops,  // [!code highlight]
		.size = obj->size,
		.flags = flags,
		.priv = obj,  // [!code highlight]
		.resv = obj->resv,
	};

	return drm_gem_dmabuf_export(dev, &exp_info);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L916-L931" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L916-L931</a> (Linux v6.19)

`dma_buf_export_info` holds <mark>ops (the dma-buf operation table)</mark> and <mark>priv = obj</mark> (the GEM object). This serves as the link connecting dma-buf back to the GEM object. The operation table is defined as follows:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_dmabuf_ops (L827-836)"
}
---
static const struct dma_buf_ops drm_gem_prime_dmabuf_ops =  {
	.attach = drm_gem_map_attach,
	.detach = drm_gem_map_detach,
	.map_dma_buf = drm_gem_map_dma_buf,  // [!code highlight]
	.unmap_dma_buf = drm_gem_unmap_dma_buf,  // [!code highlight]
	.release = drm_gem_dmabuf_release,
	.mmap = drm_gem_dmabuf_mmap,
	.vmap = drm_gem_dmabuf_vmap,
	.vunmap = drm_gem_dmabuf_vunmap,
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L827-L836" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L827-L836</a> (Linux v6.19)

`map_dma_buf` is invoked when the importer calls `dma_buf_map_attachment()`. It retrieves the page list via the GEM object's `funcs->get_sg_table()` and performs DMA mapping for the importer device.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_map_dma_buf() (L666-692)"
}
---
struct sg_table *drm_gem_map_dma_buf(struct dma_buf_attachment *attach,
				     enum dma_data_direction dir)
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj = attach->dmabuf->priv;
	struct sg_table *sgt;
	int ret;

	if (WARN_ON(dir == DMA_NONE))
		return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);

	if (WARN_ON(!obj->funcs->get_sg_table))  // [!code highlight]
		return ERR_PTR(-ENOSYS);

	sgt = obj->funcs->get_sg_table(obj);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(sgt))
		return sgt;

	ret = dma_map_sgtable(attach->dev, sgt, dir,  // [!code highlight]
			      DMA_ATTR_SKIP_CPU_SYNC);
	if (ret) {
		sg_free_table(sgt);
		kfree(sgt);
		sgt = ERR_PTR(ret);
	}

	return sgt;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L666-L692" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L666-L692</a> (Linux v6.19)

`drm_gem_prime_export()` calls `drm_gem_dmabuf_export()`, which calls `dma_buf_export()` from the dma-buf core and increments reference counts on both the device and the GEM object (ensuring the device and object are not freed while the dma-buf remains alive).  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_dmabuf_export() (L239-254)"
}
---
struct dma_buf *drm_gem_dmabuf_export(struct drm_device *dev,
				      struct dma_buf_export_info *exp_info)  // [!code highlight]
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj = exp_info->priv;
	struct dma_buf *dma_buf;

	dma_buf = dma_buf_export(exp_info);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(dma_buf))
		return dma_buf;

	drm_dev_get(dev);  // [!code highlight]
	drm_gem_object_get(obj);  // [!code highlight]
	dma_buf->file->f_mapping = obj->dev->anon_inode->i_mapping;

	return dma_buf;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L239-L254" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L239-L254</a> (Linux v6.19)

The dma-buf core implementation is as follows:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c - dma_buf_export() (L683-759)"
}
---
struct dma_buf *dma_buf_export(const struct dma_buf_export_info *exp_info)
{
	/* ... */
	if (WARN_ON(!exp_info->priv || !exp_info->ops
		    || !exp_info->ops->map_dma_buf
		    || !exp_info->ops->unmap_dma_buf
		    || !exp_info->ops->release))
		return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);
	/* ... */
	file = dma_buf_getfile(exp_info->size, exp_info->flags);  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	dmabuf = kzalloc(alloc_size, GFP_KERNEL);
	/* ... */
	dmabuf->priv = exp_info->priv;  // [!code highlight]
	dmabuf->ops = exp_info->ops;  // [!code highlight]
	dmabuf->size = exp_info->size;
	dmabuf->exp_name = exp_info->exp_name;
	dmabuf->owner = exp_info->owner;
	/* ... */
	if (!resv) {
		dmabuf->resv = (struct dma_resv *)&dmabuf[1];
		dma_resv_init(dmabuf->resv);  // [!code highlight]
	} else {
		dmabuf->resv = resv;
	}
	/* ... */
	file->private_data = dmabuf;  // [!code highlight]
	file->f_path.dentry->d_fsdata = dmabuf;
	dmabuf->file = file;

	__dma_buf_list_add(dmabuf);
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c#L683-L759" target="_blank">drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c L683-L759</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c - dma_buf_getfile() (L602-632)"
}
---
static struct file *dma_buf_getfile(size_t size, int flags)
{
	static atomic64_t dmabuf_inode = ATOMIC64_INIT(0);
	struct inode *inode = alloc_anon_inode(dma_buf_mnt->mnt_sb);  // [!code highlight]
	struct file *file;

	if (IS_ERR(inode))
		return ERR_CAST(inode);

	inode->i_size = size;
	inode_set_bytes(inode, size);

	inode->i_ino = atomic64_inc_return(&dmabuf_inode);
	flags &= O_ACCMODE | O_NONBLOCK;
	file = alloc_file_pseudo(inode, dma_buf_mnt, "dmabuf",  // [!code highlight]
				 flags, &dma_buf_fops);
	if (IS_ERR(file))
		goto err_alloc_file;

	return file;

err_alloc_file:
	iput(inode);
	return file;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c#L602-L632" target="_blank">drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c L602-L632</a> (Linux v6.19)

Here, the true nature of dma-buf is revealed: <mark>dma-buf = a pseudo file backed by an anonymous inode + operation table + private data</mark>. As a result, it can be handled via an fd, transmitted to another process via `SCM_RIGHTS`, and its reference count drops when calling `close()`. Because the file name is designated as `"dmabuf"`, it shows up in `/proc/<pid>/fd/` as follows:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c - dmabuffs_dname() (L126-140)"
}
---
static char *dmabuffs_dname(struct dentry *dentry, char *buffer, int buflen)
{
	struct dma_buf *dmabuf;
	char name[DMA_BUF_NAME_LEN];
	ssize_t ret = 0;

	dmabuf = dentry->d_fsdata;
	spin_lock(&dmabuf->name_lock);
	if (dmabuf->name)
		ret = strscpy(name, dmabuf->name, sizeof(name));
	spin_unlock(&dmabuf->name_lock);

	return dynamic_dname(buffer, buflen, "/%s:%s",  // [!code highlight]
			     dentry->d_name.name, ret > 0 ? name : "");
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c#L126-L140" target="_blank">drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf.c L126-L140</a> (Linux v6.19)

Before being named, `/proc/self/fd/N` for a dma-buf resolves to `/dmabuf:`.  


```adcard
```

#### import: dma-buf fd → handle
The receiving end calls `DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE`.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_fd_to_handle() (L292-359)"
}
---
int drm_gem_prime_fd_to_handle(struct drm_device *dev,
			       struct drm_file *file_priv, int prime_fd,
			       uint32_t *handle)
{
	/* ... */
	dma_buf = dma_buf_get(prime_fd);  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	mutex_lock(&file_priv->prime.lock);

	ret = drm_prime_lookup_buf_handle(&file_priv->prime,  // [!code highlight]
			dma_buf, handle);
	if (ret == 0)
		goto out_put;
	/* ... */
	mutex_lock(&dev->object_name_lock);
	if (dev->driver->gem_prime_import)  // [!code highlight]
		obj = dev->driver->gem_prime_import(dev, dma_buf);  // [!code highlight]
	else
		obj = drm_gem_prime_import(dev, dma_buf);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(obj)) {
		ret = PTR_ERR(obj);
		goto out_unlock;
	}
	/* ... */
	ret = drm_gem_handle_create_tail(file_priv, obj, handle);  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	ret = drm_prime_add_buf_handle(&file_priv->prime,
			dma_buf, *handle);
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L292-L359" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L292-L359</a> (Linux v6.19)

It acquires the dma-buf from the fd via `dma_buf_get(fd)`. If it is an already-known dma-buf, the existing handle is returned. If it is encountered for the first time, a GEM object is created via `drm_gem_prime_import()`, and it is registered into <b>this file's handle table</b> with `drm_gem_handle_create_tail()`. During object creation, <mark>two distinct cases</mark> exist:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c - drm_gem_prime_import_dev() (L968-1020)"
}
---
struct drm_gem_object *drm_gem_prime_import_dev(struct drm_device *dev,
					    struct dma_buf *dma_buf,
					    struct device *attach_dev)
{
	struct dma_buf_attachment *attach;  // [!code highlight]
	struct sg_table *sgt;
	struct drm_gem_object *obj;
	int ret;

	if (drm_gem_is_prime_exported_dma_buf(dev, dma_buf)) {  // [!code highlight]
		obj = dma_buf->priv;
		drm_gem_object_get(obj);
		return obj;
	}

	if (!dev->driver->gem_prime_import_sg_table)  // [!code highlight]
		return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);

	attach = dma_buf_attach(dma_buf, attach_dev);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(attach))
		return ERR_CAST(attach);

	get_dma_buf(dma_buf);

	sgt = dma_buf_map_attachment_unlocked(attach, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(sgt)) {
		ret = PTR_ERR(sgt);
		goto fail_detach;
	}

	obj = dev->driver->gem_prime_import_sg_table(dev, attach, sgt);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(obj)) {
		ret = PTR_ERR(obj);
		goto fail_unmap;
	}

	obj->import_attach = attach;  // [!code highlight]
	obj->resv = dma_buf->resv;

	return obj;

fail_unmap:
	dma_buf_unmap_attachment_unlocked(attach, sgt, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
fail_detach:
	dma_buf_detach(dma_buf, attach);
	dma_buf_put(dma_buf);

	return ERR_PTR(ret);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c#L968-L1020" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_prime.c L968-L1020</a> (Linux v6.19)

- If <b>the dma-buf was exported by the same DRM device</b> (`drm_gem_is_prime_exported_dma_buf`), <b>no new object is created</b>; it simply increments the reference count on the original object (`dma_buf->priv`). Sharing within the same device incurs virtually zero overhead.
- If <b>it was exported by a different device</b>, an sg_table is obtained via `dma_buf_attach()` → `dma_buf_map_attachment_unlocked()`, and the <mark>driver callback gem_prime_import_sg_table()</mark> instantiates its own GEM object backed by those pages. Because `obj->import_attach` is populated, the object is subsequently treated as an "imported object" (e.g., `drm_gem_dumb_map_offset()` shown earlier returns `-EINVAL` for imported objects).

#### Hands-on: Dumb Buffers and PRIME
The entire workflow above is demonstrated in a single program. A dumb buffer is created, rendered into, exported via PRIME, and imported by another `open()` call (a different `drm_file`) to read the exact same pixels.  

```c
{
    "title": "drm_dumb_prime.c - dumb buffer and PRIME",
    "ln": true
}
---
// drm_dumb_prime.c — Creates a dumb buffer, paints it via mmap, exports it via PRIME (dma-buf), and imports it on another fd.  // [!code highlight]
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <xf86drm.h>
#include <xf86drmMode.h>

static void show_fd(const char *what, int fd)
{
	char link[64], target[128] = "?";
	struct stat st;

	snprintf(link, sizeof(link), "/proc/self/fd/%d", fd);
	ssize_t n = readlink(link, target, sizeof(target) - 1);
	if (n > 0)
		target[n] = '\0';
	fstat(fd, &st);
	printf("%-12s fd=%d -> %s (inode %lu)\n", what, fd, target, (unsigned long)st.st_ino);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
	const char *path = argc > 1 ? argv[1] : "/dev/dri/card0";
	int fd = open(path, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC);

	if (fd < 0) { perror(path); return 1; }

	// 1) Create GEM object: kernel creates the object and returns a "handle valid only on this fd".
	struct drm_mode_create_dumb create = { .width = 640, .height = 480, .bpp = 32 };
	if (drmIoctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB, &create)) { perror("CREATE_DUMB"); return 1; }  // [!code highlight]
	printf("CREATE_DUMB  handle=%u pitch=%u size=%llu\n", create.handle, create.pitch,
	       (unsigned long long)create.size);

	// 2) To perform mmap, first obtain a "fake offset" (value generated by drm_vma_offset_manager).  // [!code highlight]
	struct drm_mode_map_dumb map = { .handle = create.handle };
	if (drmIoctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB, &map)) { perror("MAP_DUMB"); return 1; }  // [!code highlight]
	printf("MAP_DUMB     offset=0x%llx\n", (unsigned long long)map.offset);

	uint8_t *px = mmap(NULL, create.size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_SHARED, fd, map.offset);  // [!code highlight]
	if (px == MAP_FAILED) { perror("mmap"); return 1; }  // [!code highlight]
	for (uint32_t y = 0; y < create.height; y++)
		for (uint32_t x = 0; x < create.width; x++)
			*(uint32_t *)(px + y * create.pitch + x * 4) =
				0xff000000u | (x * 255 / create.width) << 16 | (y * 255 / create.height) << 8;

	// 3) PRIME: handle -> dma-buf fd. Exporting the same handle twice yields the exact same dma-buf.
	int prime_a, prime_b;
	if (drmPrimeHandleToFD(fd, create.handle, DRM_CLOEXEC | DRM_RDWR, &prime_a) ||  // [!code highlight]
	    drmPrimeHandleToFD(fd, create.handle, DRM_CLOEXEC | DRM_RDWR, &prime_b)) {  // [!code highlight]
		perror("PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD");
		return 1;
	}
	show_fd("export #1", prime_a);
	show_fd("export #2", prime_b);

	// 4) Import that dma-buf as a handle on another drm_file (= another open) -> handle numbers are distinct per fd.
	int fd2 = open(path, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC);
	uint32_t handle2;
	if (fd2 < 0 || drmPrimeFDToHandle(fd2, prime_a, &handle2)) { perror("PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE"); return 1; }  // [!code highlight]
	printf("import       handle=%u (fd2), exporter handle=%u (fd)\n", handle2, create.handle);

	struct drm_mode_map_dumb map2 = { .handle = handle2 };
	if (drmIoctl(fd2, DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB, &map2) == 0) {  // [!code highlight]
		uint8_t *px2 = mmap(NULL, create.size, PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED, fd2, map2.offset);  // [!code highlight]
		if (px2 != MAP_FAILED) {
			printf("pixel(100,50): exporter=0x%08x importer=0x%08x\n",
			       *(uint32_t *)(px + 50 * create.pitch + 100 * 4),
			       *(uint32_t *)(px2 + 50 * create.pitch + 100 * 4));
			munmap(px2, create.size);
		}
	} else {
		puts("(This driver does not allow MAP_DUMB on imported buffers — drm_gem_is_imported() check)");
	}

	struct drm_mode_destroy_dumb destroy = { .handle = create.handle };
	munmap(px, create.size);
	drmIoctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DESTROY_DUMB, &destroy);
	close(prime_a); close(prime_b); close(fd2); close(fd);
	return 0;
}
```
Here is how each line maps to the kernel code examined earlier:  

| User Code | What happens in the kernel |
|---|---|
| `DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB` | `drm_mode_create_dumb()` → `dumb_create` → `drm_gem_handle_create()`. The <b>first handle of a new `drm_file` is typically 1</b> (the table starts from 1) |
| `DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB` | `drm_gem_dumb_map_offset()` → vma_node address becomes the offset |
| `mmap(..., fd, offset)` | `drm_gem_mmap()` → `drm_gem_mmap_obj()` → connects pages upon page fault |
| `drmPrimeHandleToFD` ×2 | On the second call, `drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf()` returns the <b>same cached dma-buf</b> (hence the same inode) |
| `readlink("/proc/self/fd/N")` | Name of dma-buf file → `dmabuffs_dname()` → `/dmabuf:` |
| `drmPrimeFDToHandle(fd2, ...)` | Takes the <b>same-device branch</b> of `drm_gem_prime_import_dev()` → new handle is created in `fd2`'s table |

> This example has been verified to compile against `libdrm 2.4.129`. Because the environment where this post was written lacked `/dev/dri` access permissions, runtime output is not included. Running it yourself will demonstrate that both `export` operations point to the same inode, and the importer reads identical pixel values. (Note: drivers such as i915 use their own GEM implementation rather than the shmem helper, but the core parts covered in this article—the handle table and PRIME branching—proceed in the exact same way.)

