### Summary
Key takeaways from this post:  

- <b>dma-buf = a pseudo file backed by an anonymous inode + operation table + private data</b>. Being an fd, it can be passed to other processes via `SCM_RIGHTS`.
- <b>export</b> (`drm_gem_prime_handle_to_dmabuf`) searches cache first, creating a new instance only when none exists. Exporting the same handle multiple times yields the exact same dma-buf.
- <b>import</b> (`drm_gem_prime_import_dev`) reuses the <b>original object directly</b> if exported by the same device; if from a different device, it invokes `attach` → `map_attachment` to obtain an sg_table and creates an object via the driver callback. Data is never copied.

Once buffers are shared, coordination over "who is writing when" is essential. Next, we will cover <b>`dma_fence`</b>, which handles synchronization, and <b>`drm_sched`</b>, which queues and schedules GPU workloads.
